.TH TUI_BUF_INIT 3 2026-10-03 "libtuikit 0.1" "libtuikit Manual"
.SH NAME
tui_buf_init, tui_buf_free, tui_buf_reset, tui_buf_append, tui_buf_puts,
tui_buf_printf, tui_buf_write_fd \- growable byte buffer, the renderer's output
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <tuikit/buf.h>
.PP
.B typedef struct tui_buf {
.B "    char  *data;"
.B "    size_t len, cap;"
.B "    int    failed;"
.B } tui_buf;
.PP
.BI "void tui_buf_init(tui_buf *" b );
.BI "void tui_buf_free(tui_buf *" b );
.BI "void tui_buf_reset(tui_buf *" b );
.BI "int  tui_buf_append(tui_buf *" b ", const void *" p ", size_t " n );
.BI "int  tui_buf_puts(tui_buf *" b ", const char *" s );
.BI "int  tui_buf_printf(tui_buf *" b ", const char *" fmt ", ...);"
.BI "int  tui_buf_write_fd(tui_buf *" b ", int " fd );
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
Everything libtuikit draws is appended to a
.BR tui_buf .
The program, or
.BR tui_term_flush_buf (3),
writes it to a file descriptor; a test hands the same bytes to a terminal
emulator instead.
This is what makes rendering testable without a terminal.
.PP
.BR tui_buf_init ()
makes an empty buffer and allocates nothing.
.BR tui_buf_free ()
releases the memory and re-initialises the buffer.
.BR tui_buf_reset ()
empties it and clears
.IR failed ,
keeping the memory for the next frame.
.PP
.BR tui_buf_append ()
appends
.I n
bytes (NUL bytes included),
.BR tui_buf_puts ()
a NUL-terminated string, and
.BR tui_buf_printf ()
formatted text as
.BR printf (3).
The content is always followed by a NUL byte not counted in
.IR len .
.PP
An allocation failure is sticky: it sets
.IR failed ,
later appends do nothing, and
.BR tui_buf_write_fd ()
refuses to write the half-built frame, which would draw garbage.
.PP
.BR tui_buf_write_fd ()
writes the whole buffer to
.IR fd ,
retrying on
.B EINTR
and waiting with
.BR poll (2)
on
.BR EAGAIN ,
so a non-blocking descriptor loses nothing.
The buffer is emptied in every case.
.SH RETURN VALUE
The functions returning
.B int
return 0 on success and \-1 with
.I errno
set on failure:
.B EINVAL
for a NULL argument,
.B ENOMEM
for an allocation failure (or, from
.BR tui_buf_write_fd (),
for a buffer whose
.I failed
was set; nothing is written then), or the error of
.BR write (2).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR tui_screen_render (3),
.BR tui_term_open (3),
.BR libtuikit (7)
